{
  "requirement": "The `PlainText` implementation of `OutlineLang` supplies the extension, selection, and fingerprint rules described in this chapter.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub struct PlainText;\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for PlainText;\nimpl Debug for PlainText;\nimpl Default for PlainText;\nimpl OutlineLang for PlainText;\nimpl Copy for PlainText;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.sdk.sources.plain-text.PlainText` has three parts:\n- `PlainText` is a unit struct that implements `OutlineLang`.\n- It supplies the chapter's extension, selection and fingerprint rules.\n- Its declared trait impls are `Clone`, `Debug`, `Default`, `OutlineLang` and `Copy`.\n\nThe detailed rules sit in the sibling requirements `items`, `fingerprints` and `language`. They are tested under their own references. I check here only that the subject's own obligations are exercised.\n\n**Mapping to tests.**\n- **Declaration and trait impls.** The requirement's `:test:` anchor is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`.\n  - That test builds rustdoc for the crate. It calls `check_declaration`, which asserts that the expected impl set parsed from the requirement equals `rustdoc.trait_impls(page)`.\n  - Because of this, the struct declaration and all five listed trait impls (`Clone`, `Debug`, `Default`, `OutlineLang`, `Copy`) are checked against the requirement text. A missing or extra impl would fail the test.\n- **Extension rule (claims no extensions, fallback for unclaimed names).**\n  - `bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline` asserts `plain.name() == \"plain-text\"` and `plain.extensions().is_empty()`.\n  - `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` shows that `missing.unclaimed`, `README` and `missing` all get the plain-text outline.\n- **Selection rule.** `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` covers these points:\n  - The root selector is empty, and the root span covers the whole source.\n  - There is one `line` child per nonblank line, and blank lines add no children. The test checks the child count, the kinds and the text of each span.\n  - Selectors have the form `line@<12 hex digits>`, and the prefix equals the structural-hash prefix.\n  - Identical lines share a selector, and distinct lines do not.\n  - `select` resolves a line selector to the right text.\n  - Selectors stay the same after whitespace changes and blank-line changes.\n  - An empty file yields only the root.\n- **Fingerprint rule.**\n  - `plain_line_fingerprints_use_sha256_words_without_losing_boundaries` asserts the exact SHA-256 value for `hello`. It asserts that whitespace inside a line is ignored and that word boundaries matter (`two words` vs `twowords`). It also asserts the scheme string `plain-text-structural-v1:own-v1`.\n  - The root-level assertions in `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` check that reflowing and inserting blank lines preserve the root hash and fingerprint. They also check that reordering or joining lines changes the structural hash.\n- **Structural-search unsupported.** `outline_only_languages_report_unsupported_structural_search` exercises `languages::PlainText.search_structure`. The chapter states this behavior, and the test is also supplementary to the subject's scope.\n\n**Gaps considered.** `PlainText` itself is never passed through the generic `check` helper, which would exercise `Clone`, `Copy` and `Default` at runtime. The impls are derived from a zero-sized unit struct and contain no conditional logic. The rustdoc-based impl-set check also covers them, so I don't count this as a gap. I found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that the reviewed tests would accept, and no `{fixme}` is involved.",
  "references": "924e533aca72d6aa5cf27d7465ad1fab2807fa476d9cfb383046c2630041310e",
